  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nMid/upper ridging continues to hold over the eastern half of the \nCONUS, with its center over the western Great Lakes while \nsouthwesterly flow in place over the western third. Drilling down to \nthe local area, a recent surface analysis shows a complex \nenvironment, headlined by an incoming cold front to the northwest, a \nsurface low moving to farther east into central Iowa, and another \nquasi-stationary front across northeastern Kansas into central Iowa. \nThough we are to the north of the aforementioned stationary front, a \nhole in the cirrus overhead is leading to increasing\ndestabilization and warming across southeastern Nebraska amidst\nmodest lift in the lower 300 mb of the vertical column. An area\nof agitated clouds near KHSI/KGRI is likely what short-term CAM\nguidance is hinting at, where a small swath of scattered \nshowers and storms is forecast to develop and move eastward over\nthe course of the afternoon/evening. Bulk shear values of near \n20 kts and decreasing instability as surface moisture is mixed \nout will likely limit the potential of any storms that do form, \nbut a gust to 60 mph or a quarter-size hail stone are not out of\nthe question. The competing lift/subsidence of nearby \nshortwaves/MCVs will also limit the coverage of storms as well, \nwith the most likely timing being 5 PM to 9 PM across southeast \nNebraska into far southwest Iowa. \n\nHeading into the overnight hours, another area of showers and storms \nwill become possible as a MCV-driven cluster of scattered storms \nmoves across northern Kansas after 2 AM. We'll be on the side of the \nboundary that will be more hostile to storm the cluster, but it \nwon't take much wavering in the MCV's eventual path to graze \nsouthern Nebraska where marginally severe hail/wind are possible. To \nthe north of those chances, fog formation is possible and currently \nfavors northeast Nebraska into western Iowa for some patchy \nvisibility reductions. Northwesterly winds will make it hard for \nwidespread dense fog to settle in, but its patchy nature could \nsurprise some motorists getting up early tomorrow morning. \n\nFor the rest of tomorrow, we'll be squarely behind of the main \ncold front with a refreshingly cool day with plenty of sun as \nhighs top out in the upper 70s to low 80s. Any precipitation \nshould stay just to the west across the western half of \nNebraska, with a good breeze of 10-15 mph.\n\nMonday and Beyond:\n\nMonday and Tuesday see the return of strong to severe storms to the \nforecast, where post-frontal air is shunted back north and east and \ndewpoints recover back into the upper 60s to low 70s. The \nmid/upper pattern will have returned to modest zonal flow, \nhelping to increase effective shear values to around 30 kts. \nMonday's chances still look to reside in a shortwave incoming \nfrom the northwest with activity trending into late \nevening/overnight hours. That boundary layer moisture only \nincreases Tuesday ahead of the stronger cold front that will \npush through the area during the afternoon/evening hours. Out of\nthe two days, Tuesday seems to have a higher ceiling based on \nthe available convective ingredients amidst strong forcing \nassociated with the cold front, with ML guidance suggesting as \nsuch. \n\nStrong signals for severe weather taper off Wednesday through \nthe rest of the forecast period as we find ourselves on the \neastern fringe of the high-apmplitude ridge that develops to the\nwest. Temperatures currently look to stay quite comfortable in \nthe lower 80s through this period, with some minor rain chances \nas weak shortwaves or remnants of High Plains activity waft in \nfrom the northwest.",
